62	●\nQuality and operations content\n●\nDelivery methodology\nThese sections do not need to be rewritten for every project. They can be maintained as reusable\ncompany-standard content and methodologies. By contrast, technical content and\nrequirement-response sections should be written separately for each project using the RFP.\n Reusable common content + Project-specific RFP response content\nSeparating proposals in this way is expected to make automation more effective.\n6. Core AX Need 4: Standardizing Proposal and Report\nDesign\nLarge companies often establish an annual company-wide proposal design concept and template,\nthen use the same design system for all proposal materials created that year.\nThe company should eventually establish a standard design system that includes:\n●\nCover pages\n●\nBase layouts\n●\nFonts\n●\nTable design\n●\nColor usage rules\n●\nTitle hierarchy\n●\nContent placement rules\n●\nDiagram and visual styles\nA company-wide design system is not yet in place. In the longer term, however, the company\nshould first create standard templates and then have AI generate documents within those\ntemplates.\n\nEnglish translation\n4\n7. Core AX Need 5: Building a Company-Standard Glossary\nThe need to unify internal terminology, which had also been raised in previous AX sessions, was\nagain identified as important.\nDifferent authors currently use different expressions for the same concept. The company should\ncreate an official glossary and apply it to all documents.\nProposed operating model\n Register the company-standard glossary -> Upload a draft document -> AI reviews the\nfull document -> Detect nonstandard terminology -> Suggest company-standard terms\n or automatically replace them\nAutomatic replacement should not be applied indiscriminately, because context may require\ndifferent wording. The system should distinguish between review-and-suggestion mode and\nautomatic-conversion mode.\n8.	[-0.028930664,-0.060058594,-0.0065307617,-0.1